window.docContents[235].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___7___-Examples-of-algebras--Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra--Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"For every finite-dimensional simple module over the displayed algebra, there is a unique pair of field elements for which the associated auxiliary type is nonempty.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.7.4; role=supporting]\n\nFor every finite-dimensional simple module over the displayed algebra, the associated auxiliary type is nonempty for some pair of field elements.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.7.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe field-valued functions on Fin p have finrank p.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.7.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe finite-function model is a simple module over the displayed algebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.7.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe four-scalar auxiliary type is nonempty exactly when the first pair of parameters equals the second pair componentwise.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.7.4; role=supporting]\n\nA finite-dimensional simple module in prime characteristic admits parameters and a cyclically indexed basis with the stated actions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.7.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe p-th power of the first displayed generator belongs to the center in characteristic p.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.7.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe p-th power of the second displayed generator belongs to the center in characteristic p.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.7.4; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.7. Examples of algebras\u0009Representations and ideals of the Weyl algebra\u0009Representations and ideals of the Weyl algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___7___-Examples-of-algebras--Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra--Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Section-3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras-___-heading-and-setup/#Chapter3___Introduction_to_3___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.3. Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras\u0009Section 3.3: Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras — heading and setup\u0009Section 3.3: Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras — heading and setup","header":"Formalization","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Section-3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras-___-heading-and-setup/#Chapter3___Introduction_to_3___3___formalization"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem","header":"Krull-Schmidt for modules of finite length","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Irreducible-representations-of-dihedral-groups/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___12___-Problems--Irreducible-representations-of-dihedral-groups":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.12. Problems","header":"Irreducible representations of dihedral groups","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Irreducible-representations-of-dihedral-groups/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___12___-Problems--Irreducible-representations-of-dihedral-groups"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem/Maschke___s-theorem/#Chapter4___Theorem4___1___1":{"contents":"Theorem 4.1.1 (Maschke). Let G be a finite group and let k be a field whose characteristic does not divide |G|. Then:\n\n(i) The algebra k[G] is semisimple.\n\n(ii) There is an isomorphism of algebras \\psi : k[G] \\to \\bigoplus_i \\operatorname{End} V_i defined by g \\mapsto \\bigoplus_i g|_{V_i}, where V_i are the irreducible representations of G. In particular, this is an isomorphism of representations of G (where G acts on both sides by left multiplication). Hence, the regular representation k[G] decomposes into irreducibles as \\bigoplus_i \\dim(V_i) V_i, and one has the \"sum of squares formula\"\n\n|G| = \\sum_i \\dim(V_i)^2.\nProof. By Proposition 3.5.8, (i) implies (ii), and to prove (i), it is sufficient to show that if V is a finite dimensional representation of G and W \\subset V is any subrepresentation, then there exists a subrepresentation W' \\subset V such that V = W \\oplus W' as representations.\n\nChoose any complement \\widehat{W} of W in V. (Thus V = W \\oplus \\widehat{W} as vector spaces, but not necessarily as representations.) Let P be the projection along \\widehat{W} onto W, i.e., the operator on V defined by P|_W = \\operatorname{Id} and P|_{\\widehat{W}} = 0. Let\n\n\\overline{P} := \\frac{1}{|G|} \\sum_{g \\in G} \\rho(g) P \\rho(g^{-1}),\n\nwhere \\rho(g) is the action of g on V, and let\n\nW' = \\ker \\overline{P}.\n\nNow \\overline{P}|_W = \\operatorname{Id} and \\overline{P}(V) \\subseteq W, so \\overline{P}^2 = \\overline{P}, and so \\overline{P} is a projection along W'. Thus, V = W \\oplus W' as vector spaces.\n\nMoreover, for any h \\in G and any y \\in W',\n\n\\overline{P}\\rho(h)y = \\frac{1}{|G|} \\sum_{g \\in G} \\rho(g) P \\rho(g^{-1}h) y\n\n= \\frac{1}{|G|} \\sum_{\\ell \\in G} \\rho(h\\ell) P \\rho(\\ell^{-1}) y = \\rho(h) \\overline{P} y = 0,\n\nso \\rho(h)y \\in \\ker \\overline{P} = W'. Thus, W' is invariant under the action of G and is therefore a subrepresentation of V. Thus, V = W \\oplus W' is the desired decomposition into subrepresentations. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.1. Maschke's theorem\u0009Maschke's theorem","header":"Maschke's theorem","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem/Maschke___s-theorem/#Chapter4___Theorem4___1___1"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Unitary-matrix-proof-of-column-orthogonality/#Chapter4___Remark4___5___5":{"contents":"If g and h are not conjugate, this trace is clearly zero, since the matrix of the operator x \\mapsto gxh^{-1} in the basis of group elements has zero diagonal entries. On the other hand, if g and h are in the same conjugacy class, the trace is equal to the number of elements x such that x = gxh^{-1}, i.e., the order of the centralizer Z_g of g. We are done. \\square\n\nRemark 4.5.5. Another proof of this result is as follows. Consider the matrix U whose rows are labeled by irreducible representations of G and whose columns are labeled by conjugacy classes, with entries U_{V,g} = \\chi_V(g)/\\sqrt{|Z_g|}. Note that the conjugacy class of g is G/Z_g; thus |G|/|Z_g| is the number of elements conjugate to g. Thus, by Theorem 4.5.1, the rows of the matrix U are orthonormal. This means that U is unitary and hence its columns are also orthonormal, which implies the statement.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.5. Orthogonality of characters\u0009Unitary matrix proof of column orthogonality","header":"Unitary matrix proof of column orthogonality","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Unitary-matrix-proof-of-column-orthogonality/#Chapter4___Remark4___5___5"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Definition-8___1___8-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Definition-8___1___8-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.1. Projective and injective modules","header":"Definition 8.1.8 — Projective and injective objects in abelian categories","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Definition-8___1___8-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Definition-8___1___8-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Ext-functors/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Ext-functors":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.2. Tor and Ext functors","header":"Ext functors","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Ext-functors/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Ext-functors"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Linked-simple-modules-and-blocks/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___5___-Blocks--Linked-simple-modules-and-blocks":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.5. Blocks","header":"Linked simple modules and blocks","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Linked-simple-modules-and-blocks/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___5___-Blocks--Linked-simple-modules-and-blocks"}});